04:06.480 --> 04:09.800
So you might think, this looks familiar,

04:09.800 --> 04:12.040
this looks a little bit like options,

04:12.040 --> 04:14.760
and it does in the sense that it's providing you

04:14.760 --> 04:17.240
structure at how you manage your state.

04:17.240 --> 04:19.800
Because what we saw with composition API earlier is that

04:19.800 --> 04:22.520
you can wrangle together whatever you want.

04:22.520 --> 04:25.120
But this, especially when we think about global source,

04:25.120 --> 04:27.840
it's actually useful to have a convention for everyone to follow.

04:27.840 --> 04:31.200
Because you don't really want it to be like the Wild Wild West

04:31.200 --> 04:34.200
when it comes to people managing and accessing data.

04:34.200 --> 04:36.360
Something else that you might notice too though,

04:36.360 --> 04:39.040
missing from here, especially for my fellow VUX users,

04:39.040 --> 04:40.920
there are no mutations.

04:40.920 --> 04:43.800
That is probably one of the best things about Pinya,

04:43.880 --> 04:48.400
is that when we have something in here, like count zero,

04:48.400 --> 04:50.360
and we want to increment the count,

04:50.360 --> 04:53.960
all we got to do is go this.count++,

04:53.960 --> 04:56.520
and it takes care of everything for you.

04:56.520 --> 04:59.600
No more creating this separate thing that's like a mutation

04:59.600 --> 05:01.160
that requires you to call an action,

05:01.160 --> 05:03.200
and then the mutation is basically redundant

05:03.200 --> 05:04.680
of the thing that you're trying to do.

05:04.680 --> 05:07.240
That's all gone.

05:07.240 --> 05:09.560
And so we think of this another way to alias it,

05:09.560 --> 05:11.400
just to provide the mental model,

05:11.480 --> 05:15.000
data computed methods.

05:17.600 --> 05:18.800
That's what you have.

