07:51.440 --> 07:54.600
And the reason why I kind of prefaced this

07:54.600 --> 07:57.480
with how global stores are normally complicated,

07:57.480 --> 07:58.920
this should actually remind you of the composable

07:58.920 --> 08:00.440
we kind of designed ourselves.

08:00.440 --> 08:02.360
You have this thing called countStore.

08:02.360 --> 08:05.200
Again, it learns from the options API.

08:05.200 --> 08:06.720
It takes the advantage of the option API

08:06.720 --> 08:08.840
by providing some structure,

08:08.840 --> 08:11.040
gives you the chance to manipulate it as you see fit,

08:11.040 --> 08:13.480
but then when you call it, it feels just as natural

08:13.480 --> 08:15.960
as using any other part of the data.

08:15.960 --> 08:17.800
Whereas in a lot of other state managements

08:17.800 --> 08:19.240
that I've used before, there's usually a lot

08:19.240 --> 08:22.640
in terms of going in to fetch a certain thing

08:22.640 --> 08:24.760
and then auto completion becomes a bit of a pain.

08:24.760 --> 08:27.000
If you use Vuex, you knew that you had to type the string

08:27.000 --> 08:29.960
exactly a certain way to initiate a certain action.

08:29.960 --> 08:33.040
There was a lot of opportunities for mistakes.

08:33.040 --> 08:36.000
But with this, it's easy for us to understand

08:36.000 --> 08:38.120
what's happening, and on top of that,

08:38.120 --> 08:39.600
I'm always a big fan of conventions,

08:39.600 --> 08:41.320
especially when it comes to debugging.

08:42.280 --> 08:46.040
We know that state management is notoriously difficult to do.

08:46.040 --> 08:48.800
And so, if you take a look right here,

08:48.800 --> 08:51.760
you'll notice that another benefit you get by using Pinya

08:51.760 --> 08:53.680
is that you get this dev tool right here.

08:53.680 --> 08:55.600
And we can actually go ahead and start inspecting it

08:55.600 --> 08:58.120
and doing stuff just like you normally would want to do

08:58.120 --> 08:59.920
when you're debugging a large application.

08:59.920 --> 09:02.760
And the other thing that, again,

09:02.760 --> 09:04.920
it feels really natural as we do this right now,

09:04.920 --> 09:07.560
is that unlike a global state management store

09:07.560 --> 09:09.100
like the way Vuex used to be,

09:09.100 --> 09:11.560
you used to have to intentionally name space modules.

09:11.560 --> 09:12.880
In other words, you have to be like,

09:12.880 --> 09:15.520
from this global piece, only take this small piece

09:15.520 --> 09:16.480
and import it here.

09:16.480 --> 09:18.500
Only take this small piece and import it here.

09:18.500 --> 09:20.020
But then sometimes you still ended up with like,

09:20.020 --> 09:21.460
importing everything at once, right?

09:21.460 --> 09:24.100
So Vuex stores became rather cumbersome

09:24.100 --> 09:25.960
and people had trouble with that.

09:25.960 --> 09:29.580
But with this new model of like a composable about Pinya,

09:29.620 --> 09:31.960
this is actually modular by default.

09:31.960 --> 09:35.100
You only import the stores you need where you need them.

09:35.100 --> 09:37.140
And so almost without even thinking about it,

09:37.140 --> 09:38.540
you saw the performance bottleneck

09:38.540 --> 09:40.060
that might have naturally occurred

09:40.060 --> 09:42.280
in an enterprise grade app where you have the single,

09:42.280 --> 09:44.500
the singleton tracking global state,

09:44.500 --> 09:46.780
everyone just chucking stuff in there because they can

09:46.780 --> 09:48.620
or it's the easiest thing to do.

09:48.620 --> 09:51.060
But now it almost is just as easy to go,

09:51.060 --> 09:53.380
wait, my functionality is about login.

09:53.380 --> 09:54.380
I'm gonna create a login store.

09:54.380 --> 09:56.680
I'm not gonna toss it in the global state.

09:56.680 --> 09:59.380
I'm gonna actually think of it modular first.

09:59.380 --> 10:01.400
And the fact that it's named states modular first,

10:01.400 --> 10:03.260
I think that's wonderful because it encourages us

10:03.260 --> 10:05.700
to use best practices without even realizing it.

10:06.860 --> 10:09.140
And that's some of the best sort of advancements

10:09.140 --> 10:11.340
that we can have when it comes to developer toys.

